5813	Rye-Malt Concrete Sledge	5237	Specialty Grain	6.0	\N	Spicy, hoppy, and fucking brewtal. Generous rye additions create a spicy nose that is supported by touches of wheat and oats. Late Centennial hop additions along with piney American dry hopping create an intricate, syncopated rhythm to take you straight to the Cemetery Gates. A brew worthy of the Cowboys From Hell.	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1608270586620-248524c67de9?w=200&h=200&fit=crop	\N	2025-07-22 05:54:41.661028	f	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1506905925346-21bda4d32df4?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1506905925346-21bda4d32df4?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80
5818	Polish Joak	5237	Grodziskie/Gose/Lichtenhainer	3.6	\N	This is an AtG resurrection of an old Polish style called a Grätzer, which was originally made with 100% oak-smoked wheat.  We reinterpreted the recipe a little to boost the diastatic power slightly (it’s an enzymes thing) while still retaining about 66% German oak-smoked wheat.  The light body and mellow wheat flavor really allow the full smokiness to shine through.  Maxed out.ABV:  3.6%  IBU: 36  OG: 9.1P	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1608270586620-248524c67de9?w=200&h=200&fit=crop	\N	2025-07-22 05:54:41.661028	f	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1506905925346-21bda4d32df4?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1506905925346-21bda4d32df4?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80
5819	Sicherheit Wort	5237	Smoked	5.5	\N	A strong base of pilsner malt and a generous helping of German beechwood smoked malt root this beer firmly in Deutschland. A little Munich malt and a touch of sweet aromatic malt add some subtle layers of flavor, and a blend of German Magnum and Czech Saaz hops bring balancing bitterness and a touch of floral spiciness. This beer isn’t complicated. It’s just smoked and damn good.	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1608270586620-248524c67de9?w=200&h=200&fit=crop	\N	2025-07-22 05:54:41.661028	f	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1506905925346-21bda4d32df4?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1506905925346-21bda4d32df4?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80
5822	Tickle  Tuba	5237	California Common	5.5	\N	A California Common, or “steam” beer is the only style of beer indigenous to the United States. This lager has a deep copper color and a caramel malt flavor, with assertive American hop bitterness. Then to keep with the steam beer tradition we fermented with lager yeast at a warmer than normal temperature. The name 'steam' came from the fact that turn of the century West coast breweries had no way to effectively chill the boiling wort using traditional means. So they pumped the hot wort up to large, shallow, open-top bins on the roof of the brewery so that it would be rapidly chilled by the cool air blowing in off the Pacific Ocean. Thus while brewing, the brewery had a distinct cloud of steam around the roof let off by the wort as it cooled, hence the name.	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1608270586620-248524c67de9?w=200&h=200&fit=crop	\N	2025-07-22 05:54:41.661028	f	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1506905925346-21bda4d32df4?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1506905925346-21bda4d32df4?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80
