10624	Udens Molenbier	5280	Dunkelweizen	5.5	\N	Top fermenting, multi-grain beer made with barley, wheat and oats. The use of ‘caramout’ gives the beer an orange/red colour. In terms of malt this beer is close to the ‘witbier’ style. Maybe even a ‘dunkelweizen’. The yeast sort is Belgian, no herbs or spices are added and the oats give the beer an un-German feel. In short this is a beer that doesn’t easily fit into any category. Just try it and enjoy…*Translated from KBC 2011 Festival Guide. This was a test brew poured only from tap at this festival	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1608270586620-248524c67de9?w=200&h=200&fit=crop	\N	2025-07-22 05:54:43.562328	f	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1506905925346-21bda4d32df4?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1506905925346-21bda4d32df4?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80
10625	Festivalbier 2011 - Bergsch Bierbrouwersgilde	5280	Tripel	7.7	\N	Translated from Dutch.  This year a decision was made to give a young Dutch brewery the opportunity to make an easy drinking yet special beer. The Muifelbrouwerij from Berghem has made this top fermented beer using the Sint Servattumus brewing facilities. The beer was warm fermented and has additional bottle fermentation. The yeast sediment in the bottle may be served. The beer has a warm, fruity flavour and a slightly bitter finish. With an alcohol level of 7.7% it’s a solid, tasty festival beer that deserves to be served cool (7-12C).	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1608270586620-248524c67de9?w=200&h=200&fit=crop	\N	2025-07-22 05:54:43.562328	f	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1506905925346-21bda4d32df4?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1506905925346-21bda4d32df4?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80
