15892	Hugo	622	Porter	6.0	\N	Chi era Hugo? Altri non è che Hugo degli Actis, il discepolo diretto di San Silvestro Abate, patrono di Sassoferrato. Si narra che il giovane desiderasse sin dalla tenera età di prendere l’abito nero dei frati benedettini e che da quando lo indossò fu artefice di opere miracolose legate all’acqua di San Cassiano, ingrediente base delle nostra birra. Se è pur vero che l’abito non fa il monaco, bisogna ammettere che il nero sfina! La birra Hugo, di ispirazione anglosassone e alta fermentazione, si distingue per il suo carattere deciso. Al primo assaggio la sensazione di dolcezza al palato è affiancata dall’amara nota decisa dei malti tostati, che rendono la Nera una birra dal gusto elegante e pulito. Si presenta di colore marrone scuro. Schiuma beige, compatta e persistente. Al naso è complessa con aromi primari e secondari. Note di tostato, cacao amaro, caffè torrefatto, frutta secca, liquirizia. Corpo medio, complessa, persistente. Abbinamenti consigliati: dessert al cioccolato fondente o al caffè con una nota amara più che dolce. Ottima da sola come fine pasto. Servire a 10° C. Contiene glutine ed è priva di solfiti. Ingredienti: acqua San Cassiano, malto di cereali, lieviti, luppolo, zucchero.Who was Hugo? He was Hugo of Actis, the direct disciple of San Silvestro Abate, patron of Sassoferrato. It is said that the young man wanted from an early age to take the black dress of the Benedictine monks, and that since he put it was author of miraculous works related to water of San Cassiano, a basic ingredient of our beer. The beer Hugo, Anglo-Saxon inspiration and top-fermented, is distinguished by its strong character. At first taste the sensation of sweetness on the palate is accompanied by the bitter strong touch of roasted malt, which make this an elegant and clean flavor beer. It has a dark brown color. Beige, compact and persistent foam. The nose is complex with primary and secondary aromas. Toasted notes, cocoa, roasted coffee, dried fruit, liquorice. Medium-bodied, complex and persistent. Serving suggestions: dark chocolate dessert or coffee with a, more than sweet, bitter taste. Excellent alone as a dessert. Serve at 10° C. Contains gluten and is free of sulfites. Ingredients: water San Cassiano, cereal malt, yeast, hops, sugar.	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1608270586620-248524c67de9?w=200&h=200&fit=crop	\N	2025-07-22 05:54:45.430276	f	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1569529465841-dfecdab7503b?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1569529465841-dfecdab7503b?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80
13057	Sint Larius	5307	Tripel	8.8	\N	A straight up, crisp, authentic, Belgian Tripel that we brewed in conjunction with our friend, and Easton legend, Larry Porter of Porters Pub. Back in December, Larry and I took an awesome group of beer lovers to Belgium to drink, eat, and learn more about brewing. While we were there, we visited many breweries that produced super crisp, and relatively dry Tripels, that actually drank like table beers! Noting that many American versions of the style ended up finishing too sweet, we returned to the States to brew our own rendition. The yeast is really the focal point in Sint Larius and we obtained it from a very special, secluded monastic brewery in west Belgium that you may have heard of. Fruit and spice complexity run wild here, and it pours with firm carbonation and a fluffy head. The finish is just about as dry as a Tripel can be at over 8%, and I feel like one can sip on these forever...	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1608270586620-248524c67de9?w=200&h=200&fit=crop	\N	2025-07-22 05:54:44.519471	f	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1506905925346-21bda4d32df4?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1506905925346-21bda4d32df4?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80
