19037	Duality Pale Stout	715	Stout	8.5	\N	Pale Stout? Historically the word “stout” pertained more to strength than colour.  We’ve tried to pack in all the flavours usually associated with big dark beers into basically a strong English Ale. We’ve used heaps of oats for body and increased mouthfeel, smoked malt for a lovely peaty nose, cocoa nibs for that chocolaty goodness and finally added whole coffee beans from Boabab Roasters in Celbridge to round off all the flavours.Part of our initial bottle series, and part of YellowBelly Beer History.	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1608270586620-248524c67de9?w=200&h=200&fit=crop	\N	2025-07-22 05:54:46.444456	f	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1572490122747-3968b75cc699?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1572490122747-3968b75cc699?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80
19020	Great For The Town	715	Sour / Wild Ale	4.5	\N	Sour. Love. The next fruited sour from Yellowbelly showcases the magnificent Wexford delicacy, strawberries. Our house Berliner Weisse malt bill was mashed as usual, kettle soured with lactobacillus, fermented and conditioned on an absolute hape of strawberry (7kg per hl for those inclined).The resulting beer sees the usually associated mouth puckering acidity balanced out by the fruits sweetness. Lashings of fruit on the nose, on the tongue and on the finish.#GreatForTheTown	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1608270586620-248524c67de9?w=200&h=200&fit=crop	\N	2025-07-22 05:54:46.444456	f	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1551538827-9c037cb4f32d?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1551538827-9c037cb4f32d?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80
