24258	Old Parochial	5443	Scotch Ale / Wee Heavy	10.0	\N	bottled, specialBARREL AGED IMPERIAL SCOTCH ALEOur latest brew is an Imperial strength Barrel-Aged Scotch Ale. We have taken a traditional beer style and given it a modern day makeover — with a Tempest twist.Brewed back in September 2012 using smoked malted barley courtesy of Teviot Smokehouse, and fermented using two different yeast strains specially produced for this beer in our brewery, you might be able to tell we’ve given this brew some extra TLC. It’s been cold matured in the brewery for six months in tank, bourbon and sherry casks, and then lovingly blended together into a powerful contemporary ale with plenty of history.The resulting beer is huge on the nose with a hit of smoked stone fruit, wood, sweet malt, sherry and spice. Bottled and labelled by hand this ale is a rare breed indeed, and with limited availability it won’t be around for long. So get yours while you can.	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1608270586620-248524c67de9?w=200&h=200&fit=crop	\N	2025-07-22 05:54:48.295453	f	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1551538827-9c037cb4f32d?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1551538827-9c037cb4f32d?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80
24271	The Old Fashioned	5443	American Strong Ale	9.5	\N	An American original reimagined.Sit yourself down at the bar, and sip on our rye imperial pale ale aged in Heaven Hill bourbon barrels. For our latest barrel aged concoction, we decided to pay homage to one of the original, classy cocktails that would truly test our ability to balance flavours. What’s more, it’s in 660ml bottles, hand labelled, and wax dipped for a beer sure to impress.The Old Fashioned takes our well-loved orange and ginger double IPA, and infuses it with the smooth, warming, vanilla character of Heaven Hill’s famous Kentucky straight bourbon. That whiskey warmth and flavour brings out the sweeter notes in the orange and ginger, but the hops give that distinct bitterness you’d expect in the cocktail itself. It has also been conditioned in bottle, allowing it to develop a rich, smooth texture that goes down a treat.Brewers Note: the Old Fashioned’s base is a pale ale with lots of hops and citrus fruits. It will not benefit from extended aging; we have matured it in barrels for you already. Drink now for maximum freshness and enjoyment.	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1608270586620-248524c67de9?w=200&h=200&fit=crop	\N	2025-07-22 05:54:48.295453	f	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1551538827-9c037cb4f32d?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1551538827-9c037cb4f32d?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80
24273	Harvest Ipa - Mosaic Saison	5443	Belgian Ale	6.7	\N	The first in a new series showcasing our favourite Belgian yeast strain and hops to shine with it, the Harvest IPA: Mosaic.  This is one of our favourite North American hops used as a blend in the Armadillo and our black IPA (rich in citrus, earthy pine, tropical fruit, herbal and stone fruit notes). The purists may well wonder what on earth a Belgian IPA is supposed to be?? And we might wonder the same. However, this was a no brainer in terms of building a beer with a tonne of flavour, derived from a distinct yeast character and generous amount of dry hopping.‘Juicy’ comes to mind when describing this beer. We allowed the beer to free rise in fermentation. This means rather than fermenting at a controlled temperature (usually around 20 degrees c), the yeast is allowed to go crazy, climbing up to around 30 degrees c  – the idea here being that a warmer fermentation produces much more of the fruit and spice esters that characterise a Saison yeast. It also helps to produce a dry finish in the beer (1.009) which ultimately brings a nice bit of balance to those big, juicy fruit flavours. Mosaic works beautifully well in this beer; favoured this time for its heavily aromatic character but with a much more rounded fruit profile (think tropical, soft fruits, berry etc) than most big American hop varieties. As for the malt –  nice and light, a bit of oats and wheat for mouthfeel and head retention, but kept simple enough to let the hops and yeast do most of the talking. Dangerously drinkable at 6.7%…	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1608270586620-248524c67de9?w=200&h=200&fit=crop	\N	2025-07-22 05:54:48.295453	f	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1551538827-9c037cb4f32d?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80	https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1551538827-9c037cb4f32d?ixlib=rb-4.0.3&auto=format&fit=crop&w=800&q=80
